# Project: Robot Framework Test Automation

## Framework Structure
- Use Robot Framework as the keyword-driven test automation platform
- Organize the project as: tests/, resources/, libraries/, results/
- Store reusable keywords in resources/ as .robot files, custom libraries in libraries/ as Python classes
- Use separate variable files (.py or .yaml) for environment-specific configuration

## Coding Standards
- Follow Robot Framework's 4-space indentation convention strictly
- Use Title Case for keyword names (e.g., Login With Valid Credentials)
- Use UPPER_CASE for variable names (e.g., ${BASE_URL}, ${TIMEOUT})
- Limit each keyword to a single, well-defined action; keep them short and composable

## Test Organization
- Name test files descriptively: feature_name_tests.robot
- Use Tags to categorize tests: smoke, regression, integration, slow
- Group related test cases inside one .robot file with a clear *** Settings *** and *** Test Cases *** section
- Use *** Keywords *** section within a test file only for file-local helpers; shared keywords go to resources/

## Best Practices
- Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern using Setup, action keywords, and Then-style assertions
- Write test cases in a human-readable, Given-When-Then style for acceptance tests
- Keep test cases short (3–10 lines); delegate detail to resource keywords
- Avoid hardcoded values in test cases; use variables and variable files instead
- Separate test logic from technical implementation (page actions, API calls) in keyword libraries

## Keyword Design
- Name keywords as natural-language phrases that describe intent, not implementation
- Build keyword hierarchies: high-level test keywords call mid-level action keywords call low-level technical keywords
- Use embedded arguments (e.g., User "${user}" Logs In) for readable parametrization
- Tag keywords with [Tags] and document them with [Documentation] for maintainability
- Avoid duplicating keywords; prefer importing shared resources via Resource in *** Settings ***

## Variable Management
- Define global variables in a dedicated variables.py or variables.yaml file
- Use ${} for scalar, @{} for list, and &{} for dictionary variables
- Override variables at runtime via --variable or --variablefile CLI flags for environment switching
- Store sensitive values (credentials, API keys) in external variable files excluded from version control

## Library Usage
- Import only the libraries actually needed (SeleniumLibrary, RequestsLibrary, etc.)
- Pin library versions in requirements.txt for reproducible environments
- Wrap complex third-party library interactions in custom Python keyword libraries
- Use SeleniumLibrary's built-in waiting mechanisms (Wait Until Element Is Visible) instead of Sleep

## Web Testing (SeleniumLibrary)
- Use the Page Object pattern: encapsulate page-specific keywords and locators in resource files per page
- Define all locators as variables at the top of each resource file for easy maintenance
- Prefer CSS selectors or data-testid attributes over XPath for locator stability
- Always use explicit waits; never use Sleep for synchronization

## API Testing (RequestsLibrary)
- Create a reusable session in a Suite Setup keyword and close it in Suite Teardown
- Store base URLs, headers, and auth tokens as suite-level variables
- Validate response status codes and body with dedicated assertion keywords
- Log request and response details for diagnostics using Log To Console or BuiltIn.Log

## Reporting
- Use the built-in output.xml, log.html, and report.html generated by Robot Framework
- Integrate with CI by exiting with the Robot return code and archiving results/
- Use pabot (parallel Robot Framework executor) for large suites to reduce wall-clock time
- Add --listener for real-time test result streaming to external dashboards

## CI/CD Integration
- Run tests with: robot --outputdir results --loglevel INFO tests/
- Use --include tag and --exclude tag to select test subsets in different pipeline stages
- Upload results/ as a CI artifact for post-run review
- Combine with tox or Makefile targets for consistent local and CI execution

## Error Handling
- Use Run Keyword And Expect Error for tests that verify error conditions
- Add [Teardown] to each test to ensure cleanup even on failure
- Use Suite Setup / Suite Teardown for expensive shared state (browser sessions, DB connections)
- Enable screenshot-on-failure via SeleniumLibrary's register_keyword_to_run_on_failure

## Documentation
- Add [Documentation] to every test case and public keyword describing its purpose
- Maintain a resources/README.md listing available resource files and their responsibilities
- Document custom Python libraries with standard Python docstrings
- Include setup instructions (virtualenv, pip install -r requirements.txt, browser driver) in the project README

Remember to keep Robot Framework tests readable by non-technical stakeholders — the keyword layer exists precisely to bridge business language and technical automation.
